{# ADD SOMEBODY — BOTH WAYS SHIP, side by side.

   They are not two settings and not a migration: they are two answers to a real
   difference, which is whether the person is standing next to you. #}
{% extends '@UhifadhiShell/page.html.twig' %}
{% import '@UhifadhiTeam/team/_bits.html.twig' as bits %}

{% block title %}Add somebody &middot; team{% endblock %}

{% block stylesheets %}
    {{ parent() }}
    {{ include('@UhifadhiTeam/_stylesheets.html.twig') }}
{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_tabs %}{% endblock %}

{% block shell_breadcrumbs %}
    <a href="{{ path('team_index') }}">uhifadhi</a> / <a href="{{ path('team_index') }}">team</a> / add somebody
{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_title %}Add somebody{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_subtitle %}
    <b>Both ways ship.</b> Creating somebody with a password works from day one and needs no infrastructure at
    all; inviting them by email ships beside it and starts working by itself the moment an installation has a
    mailer configured. They are not two settings and not a migration &mdash; they are two answers to a real
    difference, which is whether the person is standing next to you.
{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_actions %}
    <a class="tgl w-act" href="{{ path('team_index') }}">{{ bits.icon_back() }} Back to the team</a>
{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page %}

{# THE INVITE PATH AS FOUR STEPS, so the second and third are not a surprise. #}
<div class="iv-flow">
    <div class="iv-step on"><span class="n">step 1</span><b>An administrator adds them</b><span class="rt">/team/invite</span></div>
    <div class="iv-step"><span class="n">step 2</span><b>The account exists, unverified</b><span class="rt">team_user.isVerified = false</span></div>
    <div class="iv-step"><span class="n">step 3</span><b>They set their own password</b><span class="rt">/invite/{token}</span></div>
    <div class="iv-step"><span class="n">step 4</span><b>They sign in</b><span class="rt">/login</span></div>
</div>
<p class="pgsub" style="margin:-10px 0 20px">That is the <b>invite</b> path. The <b>create</b> path is the same
flow with steps 2 and 3 collapsed into step 1: the administrator sets the password, so the account is verified the
moment it exists and the person goes straight to signing in.</p>

<div class="iv-ways">

    {# ── CREATE WITH A PASSWORD ───────────────────────────────────────── #}
    <div class="iv-way">
        <div class="iv-whead"><b>Create with a password</b><span class="chip ok">always available</span></div>
        <div class="iv-wbody">
            <p class="iv-lede">The administrator types the person&rsquo;s details and a first password, and hands
            it over. <b>This path needs nothing at all from the deployment</b> &mdash; no mailer, no network, no
            working inbox &mdash; which is exactly why it is the one that is always available. The person is
            verified the moment they exist, because an administrator who typed the password has already proved the
            account is real. It is the right path when the two of you are in the same room, and it is the only
            path when a ranger&rsquo;s only address is one they cannot reach from the field.</p>

            <form method="post" action="{{ path('team_member_create') }}">
                <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ createToken }}">
                <div class="mb-form">
                    <div class="mb-f"><span class="lbl">first name</span><input class="fld" name="firstName" placeholder="Joseph" required></div>
                    <div class="mb-f"><span class="lbl">last name</span><input class="fld" name="lastName" placeholder="Mrema" required></div>
                    <div class="mb-f"><span class="lbl">email</span><input class="fld" name="email" type="email" placeholder="j.mrema@example.test" required></div>
                    <div class="mb-f"><span class="lbl">ranger code &mdash; optional</span><input class="fld" name="rangerCode" placeholder="R-121"></div>
                    <div class="mb-f wide">
                        <span class="lbl">first password</span>
                        <input class="fld" type="password" name="password" minlength="{{ passwordMinLength }}" required autocomplete="new-password">
                        <p class="hint">At least {{ passwordMinLength }} characters. Hashed on save. <b>The product
                        cannot show it again</b>, so the administrator has to hand it over now &mdash; which is
                        this way&rsquo;s real cost, and it is operational rather than technical.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="mb-f wide">
                        <span class="lbl">position</span>
                        <select class="sel" name="position" style="height:34px;max-width:340px">
                            <option value="">&mdash; no position &mdash;</option>
                            {% for department, positions in groupedPositions %}
                                <optgroup label="{{ department is empty ? 'No department yet' : department }}">
                                    {% for position in positions %}<option value="{{ position.uuidString }}">{{ position.name }}</option>{% endfor %}
                                </optgroup>
                            {% endfor %}
                        </select>
                        <p class="hint">Grouped by department, because a position&rsquo;s name is unique only
                        inside one. Optional, and honestly so: leaving it empty creates somebody who can sign in
                        and do nothing. Better than guessing.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="mb-f wide"><button class="cta" type="submit">{{ bits.icon_plus() }} Create the account</button></div>
                </div>
            </form>

            <div class="iv-cost">
                <span class="lbl">what this path needs</span>
                <ul>
                    <li class="have">{{ bits.icon_check() }}<span>Nothing from the deployment. No mailer, no outbound network, no reachable inbox.</span></li>
                    <li class="have">{{ bits.icon_check() }}<span>The password hasher &mdash; already part of the firewall.</span></li>
                    <li class="need">{{ bits.icon_warn() }}<span>A habit: the password is hashed on save and <b>the product can never show it again</b>, so it has to be handed over in the room.</span></li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# ── INVITE BY EMAIL ──────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
    <div class="iv-way" data-mailer="{{ mailerConfigured ? 'on' : 'off' }}">
        <div class="iv-whead">
            <b>Invite by email</b>
            {% if mailerConfigured %}
                <span class="chip ok">available &mdash; mailer configured</span>
            {% else %}
                <span class="chip idle">unavailable here</span>
            {% endif %}
        </div>
        <div class="iv-wbody">
            <p class="iv-lede">The administrator types one thing &mdash; an address &mdash; and the person sets
            their own password from a link. <b>Nobody ever knows anybody else&rsquo;s password</b>, which is the
            whole argument for it, and it is the right path for anybody who is not in the room. It needs one thing
            from the deployment: somewhere to send mail from.</p>

            {% if not mailerConfigured %}
                {# OFFERED AND REFUSED, NOT HIDDEN. The rule against disabled
                   controls is for a thing that will NEVER exist; this one is an
                   environment variable away, and the rule's own wording allows
                   the exception. #}
                <div class="iv-nomailer">
                    {{ bits.icon_warn() }}
                    <div><b>This installation has no mailer configured, so this path is offered and refused &mdash;
                    not hidden.</b> <code>MAILER_DSN</code> is unset. Hiding the option would leave an
                    administrator hunting for a feature the product does have; failing silently after the click
                    would leave a colleague waiting for an email nobody sent. So the form is visible, the button
                    is inert, and the reason is written on it. Configure a transport and this path starts working
                    with no other change. Meanwhile the path on the left needs nothing, which is the point of
                    shipping both.</div>
                </div>
            {% endif %}

            <form method="post" action="{{ path('team_invite_send') }}">
                <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ inviteToken }}">
                <div class="mb-form">
                    <div class="mb-f wide">
                        <span class="lbl">email</span>
                        <input class="fld" name="email" type="email" placeholder="h.rajabu@example.test" {{ mailerConfigured ? 'required' : 'disabled' }}>
                        <p class="hint">One field, so it is one field &mdash; not a screen. The name comes from
                        the person when they accept; an administrator guessing at somebody&rsquo;s own spelling of
                        their own name is a small indignity the product does not need to cause.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="mb-f wide">
                        <span class="lbl">position &mdash; optional</span>
                        <select class="sel" name="position" style="height:34px;max-width:340px" {{ mailerConfigured ? '' : 'disabled' }}>
                            <option value="">&mdash; decide later &mdash;</option>
                            {% for department, positions in groupedPositions %}
                                <optgroup label="{{ department is empty ? 'No department yet' : department }}">
                                    {% for position in positions %}<option value="{{ position.uuidString }}">{{ position.name }}</option>{% endfor %}
                                </optgroup>
                            {% endfor %}
                        </select>
                    </div>
                    <div class="mb-f wide">
                        <button class="cta" type="submit" {{ mailerConfigured ? '' : 'disabled title="No mail transport is configured, so there is nothing to send the invitation with."' }}>
                            {{ bits.icon_plus() }} Send the invitation
                        </button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </form>

            <div class="iv-cost">
                <span class="lbl">what this path needs</span>
                <ul>
                    <li class="have">{{ bits.icon_check() }}<span><code>verificationToken</code> and <code>isVerified</code> &mdash; both columns already exist and are the natural hook.</span></li>
                    <li class="{{ mailerConfigured ? 'have' : 'need' }}">{{ mailerConfigured ? bits.icon_check() : bits.icon_warn() }}<span><b>A configured mailer.</b> The one deployment-level requirement, and the only reason this path is ever unavailable.</span></li>
                    <li class="have">{{ bits.icon_check() }}<span><code>invitedAt</code> and <code>invitedBy</code> &mdash; so the roster can say who invited somebody and when, instead of only that they have not arrived.</span></li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="okline" style="margin-top:20px">
    {{ bits.icon_info() }}
    <span><b>Both ship, and the cost of that is honest:</b> an account can be born in two states, and the team
    list has to read correctly for both forever. It does, and the two facts are kept apart to make that work: the
    state a row shows is <b><code>isVerified</code></b> either way &mdash; which is why &ldquo;never signed
    in&rdquo; is the pill on both &mdash; and <b><code>invitedAt</code> / <code>invitedBy</code></b> ride beside it
    as context where they exist. An account made on the left has neither, and says so by saying nothing.</span>
</div>
{% endblock %}
